1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,992
This is ECE 320, video 22b. The topic is cache coherence: the protocols that keep private caches correct. It continues video 22a.

2
00:00:13,442 --> 00:00:21,741
After this video, you can do four things. One: explain what a coherence controller does, and the events it reacts to.

3
00:00:22,191 --> 00:00:29,209
Two: use the VI, MSI and MESI tables to find the next state of a cache block.

4
00:00:29,659 --> 00:00:36,891
Three: trace a snooping bus, step by step, and give the states, the data and memory after each operation.

5
00:00:37,341 --> 00:00:45,491
Four: trace a directory protocol, with its owner and its sharers, and explain the O state of MOESI.

6
00:00:45,941 --> 00:00:57,247
Video 22a ended with this system. Each core has a private write-back cache. P1 stored 400 to A, so P1's copy is dirty.

7
00:00:57,697 --> 00:01:06,188
The shared cache and memory still hold 500 for A. A write-back cache updates the next level only when it evicts the block.

8
00:01:06,638 --> 00:01:12,846
Now P0 loads A. P0's cache does not hold A, so the load is a miss.

9
00:01:13,296 --> 00:01:16,496
The miss goes over the interconnect to the shared cache.

10
00:01:16,946 --> 00:01:22,151
The shared cache holds 500 for A, so it sends 500 to P0.

11
00:01:22,601 --> 00:01:31,028
But the last write to A was 400, in P1's cache. P0 read an old value. The caches are incoherent.

12
00:01:31,478 --> 00:01:46,134
Video 22a showed that no caches, uncachable shared data, or flushing all caches are too slow. The solution is hardware cache coherence: all caches have the same data at all times.

13
00:01:46,584 --> 00:01:58,445
The coherence goal says that all caches have the same data at all times. Simple multiprocessors reach it with a bus. Each processor has a private cache on the bus.

14
00:01:58,895 --> 00:02:02,842
P0 puts a request on the bus: a read of block A.

15
00:02:03,292 --> 00:02:09,116
The bus is one shared set of wires. So every cache sees the request at the same time.

16
00:02:09,566 --> 00:02:18,099
A second request, from P3, must wait until the bus is free. So every cache sees the two requests in the same order.

17
00:02:18,549 --> 00:02:25,163
The rule: on a bus, all processors see all requests at the same time, and in the same order.

18
00:02:25,613 --> 00:02:34,914
Coherence answers one question: how do other CPUs see a memory update? All copies must have the same data at all times.

19
00:02:35,364 --> 00:02:44,132
Each cache gets a coherence controller, C C. It examines the bus traffic: addresses and data. This is called snooping.

20
00:02:44,582 --> 00:02:54,011
The controller executes the coherence protocol. The protocol says what to do with the local copy, for each event that the controller sees.

21
00:02:54,461 --> 00:03:07,901
The protocol keeps two rules. The first is SWMR: single writer, multiple readers. For a location A, at one time, only one core may write A. It may also read A.

22
00:03:08,351 --> 00:03:12,895
At another time, some number of cores may read A, but none may write it.

23
00:03:13,345 --> 00:03:26,764
The second rule is DVI, the data-value invariant. A read returns the value of the last write. Here P0 wrote 400, so every later read returns 400.

24
00:03:27,214 --> 00:03:39,801
The controller reacts to events. Three events come from the local processor: Load, Store, and Repl. Repl is a replacement: the cache evicts a block, with a write-back.

25
00:03:40,251 --> 00:03:47,227
A local event can put a request on the bus. A load miss puts Own GetS. A store puts Own GetM.

26
00:03:47,677 --> 00:03:52,541
An eviction puts Put. It is PutS or PutM, for the state of the block.

27
00:03:52,991 --> 00:04:02,761
Two events come from other processors. OtherGetS is a read miss of another processor. OtherGetM is a write miss of another processor.

28
00:04:03,211 --> 00:04:11,873
One bus request has two names. P0's GetS is Own GetS for P0, and OtherGetS for every other cache.

29
00:04:12,323 --> 00:04:21,432
So each protocol is a table. A state and an event give an action and a next state. Every question in this video uses this table.

30
00:04:21,882 --> 00:04:37,349
The first protocol is VI, valid-invalid. It is also called MI. Each block in a cache is in one of two states. V means the cache has the block. I means it does not. One valid bit can hold this state.

31
00:04:37,799 --> 00:04:44,796
A local load or store needs the block. So from I, the cache issues Get on the bus, and goes to V.

32
00:04:45,246 --> 00:04:51,305
In V, a load or a store hits. The block is already here, so nothing goes on the bus.

33
00:04:51,755 --> 00:05:04,576
When another processor wants to read or write the block, this cache gives it up. It goes to I, and sends its data if the copy is dirty. A replacement also goes to I, with a write-back.

34
00:05:05,026 --> 00:05:10,957
In I, requests from other processors need no action, because the cache has no copy.

35
00:05:11,407 --> 00:05:18,916
This is an invalidate protocol: when another processor wants the block, the local copy becomes invalid.

36
00:05:19,366 --> 00:05:28,305
The same protocol, as a table. Rows are states. Columns are events. Each cell gives the action, a slash, and the next state.

37
00:05:28,755 --> 00:05:35,795
State I, Load or Store: issue Get, then go to V. Events from other processors: nothing to do.

38
00:05:36,245 --> 00:05:38,783
State V, Load or Store: a hit.

39
00:05:39,233 --> 00:05:43,457
State V, Repl: issue Put with the data, then go to I.

40
00:05:43,907 --> 00:05:51,929
State V, OtherGetS or OtherGetM: send the data, then go to I. Each cell matches one edge of the diagram.

41
00:05:52,379 --> 00:06:00,933
Send Data puts the block on the bus, for memory and for the requester. Memory sends the data when no processor responds.

42
00:06:01,383 --> 00:06:10,749
The memory controller has its own table. It must remember the state of each cache line: I, no cache has it, or V, one cache has it.

43
00:06:11,199 --> 00:06:18,281
State I, bus event Get: no cache can respond, so memory sends the data. The line goes to V.

44
00:06:18,731 --> 00:06:25,515
State V, Put: a cache writes the block back. Memory updates the block, and the line goes to I.

45
00:06:25,965 --> 00:06:33,880
State V, Send Data: one cache sends the block to another. Memory updates its copy too, and the line stays V.

46
00:06:34,330 --> 00:06:43,845
Here is the bank example of video 22a, now with the VI protocol. Both caches start in I. Memory holds 500.

47
00:06:44,295 --> 00:06:56,007
Processor 0 runs load word. Its cache is in I, and the event is Load: issue Get, go to V. No other cache has A, so memory sends 500.

48
00:06:56,457 --> 00:07:06,163
Processor 0 stores 400. Its cache is in V, so the store hits. Memory keeps 500, because the cache is write-back.

49
00:07:06,613 --> 00:07:17,237
Processor 1 runs load word and issues Get. For processor 0 this is an OtherGetS. Processor 0 is in V: send data, go to I.

50
00:07:17,687 --> 00:07:23,362
The data goes to processor 1. CPU 1 now holds V with 400.

51
00:07:23,812 --> 00:07:28,527
Send Data also goes to memory, so memory now holds 400.

52
00:07:28,977 --> 00:07:40,518
Processor 1 stores 300. It is in V, so the store hits. The balance is correct: 500 minus 100 minus 100 is 300.

53
00:07:40,968 --> 00:07:47,517
So VI keeps at most one cached copy. Every new reader takes the block away from the last one.

54
00:07:47,967 --> 00:07:56,650
VI is inefficient. It allows only one cached copy of a block in the whole system. Here P0 holds A in V.

55
00:07:57,100 --> 00:08:03,671
P1 reads A. P0 must give the block up, so P1 now holds the only copy.

56
00:08:04,121 --> 00:08:16,046
P2 reads A, and P1 gives it up. Multiple copies cannot exist, even when all processors only read. This is a big problem in real programs.

57
00:08:16,496 --> 00:08:24,987
The solution splits V into two states. M, modified: a local dirty copy. S, shared: a local clean copy.

58
00:08:25,437 --> 00:08:31,474
Then a block has either many read-only copies in S, or one read-write copy in M.

59
00:08:31,924 --> 00:08:37,343
MSI has three states: I, invalid; S, shared; and M, modified.

60
00:08:37,793 --> 00:08:43,211
From I, a load issues GetS and gets a clean copy. The block goes to S.

61
00:08:43,661 --> 00:08:49,059
From I, a store issues GetM and gets the only copy. The block goes to M.

62
00:08:49,509 --> 00:08:57,957
In S, a load hits. A read from another processor, OtherGetS, is also fine: the copy stays clean and shared.

63
00:08:58,407 --> 00:09:05,873
In S, a store needs write permission. The cache issues GetM, and goes to M. This is called an upgrade.

64
00:09:06,323 --> 00:09:18,227
In S, OtherGetM means another processor will write, so the copy becomes invalid. A replacement also goes to I, with no write-back, because the copy is clean.

65
00:09:18,677 --> 00:09:23,200
In M, loads and stores hit. This cache has the only copy.

66
00:09:23,650 --> 00:09:32,205
In M, OtherGetS means another processor wants to read. The cache sends its dirty data, and keeps a clean copy in S.

67
00:09:32,655 --> 00:09:40,911
In M, OtherGetM sends the data and goes to I. A replacement writes the block back with PutM, and goes to I.

68
00:09:41,361 --> 00:09:44,945
In I, requests from other processors need no action.

69
00:09:45,395 --> 00:09:53,309
Here is the MSI table. It holds the same information as the diagram: one cell for each state and event.

70
00:09:53,759 --> 00:10:02,975
Row I. Load issues GetS and goes to S. Store issues GetM and goes to M. Events from other processors: nothing to do.

71
00:10:03,425 --> 00:10:08,887
Row S. A load hits. A store issues GetM and goes to M: the upgrade.

72
00:10:09,337 --> 00:10:19,470
S with Repl goes to I with no action, because the copy is clean. S with OtherGetM goes to I. S with OtherGetS needs nothing.

73
00:10:19,920 --> 00:10:22,395
Row M. Loads and stores hit.

74
00:10:22,845 --> 00:10:32,935
M with Repl issues PutM and goes to I. OtherGetS sends the data and goes to S. OtherGetM sends the data and goes to I.

75
00:10:33,385 --> 00:10:41,108
The upgrade: a local store to a shared block sends the other S copies to I. The local copy goes to M.

76
00:10:41,558 --> 00:10:50,347
The main question type gives the caches and memory, then one CPU operation. You give the new states, the data and memory.

77
00:10:50,797 --> 00:10:58,115
Step 1: find the cache line of the address, and check hit or miss. A block in state I counts as a miss.

78
00:10:58,565 --> 00:11:06,138
Step 2: if the line holds another valid block, replace it first. A block in M is written back with PutM.

79
00:11:06,588 --> 00:11:13,159
Step 3: read the requester's table cell. It gives the bus request: GetS, GetM, or none.

80
00:11:13,609 --> 00:11:22,505
Step 4: every other cache that holds the block sees OtherGetS or OtherGetM. Read its cell. A cache in M sends the data.

81
00:11:22,955 --> 00:11:30,187
Step 5: fill the line, then write the store value. When a cache sends data, memory takes a copy too.

82
00:11:30,637 --> 00:11:45,826
Here is the slide example. P1 holds A in state M, with 500. The shared cache and memory hold an old value, 1000. The shared cache state, Modified, records that a core has the dirty copy.

83
00:11:46,276 --> 00:11:53,785
P0 loads A. P0's cache has no copy of A, so the load misses. That is step 1 of the method.

84
00:11:54,235 --> 00:12:03,174
P0 is in I, and the event is Load. The cell says: issue GetS. The request, GetS for address A, goes on the bus.

85
00:12:03,624 --> 00:12:13,373
Every controller on the bus sees the GetS. For P1 and P2, it is an OtherGetS. P2 has no copy, so P2 does nothing.

86
00:12:13,823 --> 00:12:22,698
P1 holds A in M. The cell M, OtherGetS says: send data, go to S. P1 follows the edge from M to S.

87
00:12:23,148 --> 00:12:28,716
P1 puts its data on the bus: response, address A, data 500.

88
00:12:29,166 --> 00:12:35,011
The block travels to P0. P0's line now holds A, with 500.

89
00:12:35,461 --> 00:12:42,459
P0's own cell, I with Load, ends in S. So P0 follows the edge from I to S.

90
00:12:42,909 --> 00:12:48,285
Send Data also goes to the memory side, so the shared cache takes 500.

91
00:12:48,735 --> 00:12:58,185
Two cores now share A, and the shared cache differs from memory. So its state becomes Shared, Dirty. Memory still holds 1000.

92
00:12:58,635 --> 00:13:03,883
The load completes. P0 reads 500, the value of the last write.

93
00:13:04,333 --> 00:13:13,400
Next, P0 stores 400 to A. P0 holds A in S, so it may not write yet. S with Store is the upgrade.

94
00:13:13,850 --> 00:13:20,869
The cell S, Store says: issue GetM, go to M. P0 puts GetM, for address A, on the bus.

95
00:13:21,319 --> 00:13:26,417
The other controllers see an OtherGetM. P2 has no copy, and does nothing.

96
00:13:26,867 --> 00:13:34,355
P1 holds A in S. The cell S, OtherGetM says: go to I. P1's copy becomes invalid.

97
00:13:34,805 --> 00:13:43,019
The shared cache sees the GetM too. One core will hold the only dirty copy, so its state becomes Modified, Dirty.

98
00:13:43,469 --> 00:13:53,154
Now P0 is the only holder. P0 follows the edge from S to M. It needs no data, because its copy is already valid.

99
00:13:53,604 --> 00:14:01,839
The store writes 400 into P0's line. The shared cache and memory keep old values, until a write-back.

100
00:14:02,289 --> 00:14:15,686
Before: P1 held A in M. After: P0 holds A in M with 400, and P1 is invalid. A read shares the dirty block. A write invalidates the other copies.

101
00:14:16,136 --> 00:14:26,760
Assignment 8, question 1 a. The caches use MSI on a snooping bus. Each cache is direct-mapped, with four blocks, B0 to B3.

102
00:14:27,210 --> 00:14:34,933
Each block holds two 4-byte words. The figure shows two hex digits per word. The low word is on the right.

103
00:14:35,383 --> 00:14:50,871
To find the block of an address, split its bits. Address 120 is 0001 0010 0000. A block is 8 bytes, so the low 3 bits are the offset.

104
00:14:51,321 --> 00:15:01,731
Four blocks need 2 index bits: the next two bits. For 120 they are 0 0, so address 120 goes to block B0.

105
00:15:02,181 --> 00:15:11,013
The same split gives the block of every address. 110 and 130 both go to B2, so they compete for one line.

106
00:15:11,463 --> 00:15:23,175
Each part starts again from this state. The answer lists each changed block as P i dot B j, state, address, data. A memory change is M, address, data.

107
00:15:23,625 --> 00:15:30,239
Pause the video and try it. Part 2: P0 writes 80 to address 120.

108
00:15:30,689 --> 00:15:43,083
Step 1: address 120 goes to block B0. P0's B0 holds 100, in state I. So the write misses, and nothing needs a write-back.

109
00:15:43,533 --> 00:15:53,475
Step 3: P0's cell, I with Store, says: issue GetM. GetM 120 goes on the bus. Every other cache sees an OtherGetM.

110
00:15:53,925 --> 00:16:08,282
Step 4: P15's B0 holds 120 in S. The cell S, OtherGetM says: go to I. P1's B0 holds another block, 100, so P1 does nothing.

111
00:16:08,732 --> 00:16:20,039
No cache holds 120 in M, so memory sends the block, 00 20. It fills P0's B0, with address 120.

112
00:16:20,489 --> 00:16:23,305
P0 follows the edge from I to M.

113
00:16:23,755 --> 00:16:32,736
The store writes 80 into the word at 120, the low word on the right. B0 holds 00 80.

114
00:16:33,186 --> 00:16:47,501
Answer: P0 dot B0 is M, 120, 00 80. P15 dot B0 is I, 120, 00 20. Memory does not change.

115
00:16:47,951 --> 00:16:53,497
Pause the video and try it. Part 4: P1 reads address 110.

116
00:16:53,947 --> 00:17:03,825
Step 1: 110 goes to block B2. P1's B2 holds 110, but in state I. An invalid block is a miss.

117
00:17:04,275 --> 00:17:13,149
Step 3: P1's cell, I with Load, says: issue GetS. GetS 110 goes on the bus. The others see an OtherGetS.

118
00:17:13,599 --> 00:17:29,215
Step 4: P0's B2 holds 110 in M, with 00 30. The cell M, OtherGetS says: send data, go to S. P15's B2 is invalid, so P15 does nothing.

119
00:17:29,665 --> 00:17:35,617
P0 sends its block, 00 30. It fills P1's B2.

120
00:17:36,067 --> 00:17:38,606
P1 follows the edge from I to S.

121
00:17:39,056 --> 00:17:47,931
Send Data also goes to memory. Memory 110 changes from 00 10 to 00 30.

122
00:17:48,381 --> 00:18:04,359
Answer: P0 dot B2 is S, 110, 00 30. P1 dot B2 is S, 110, 00 30. Memory 110 is 00 30.

123
00:18:04,809 --> 00:18:11,380
Pause the video and try it. Part 6: P0 writes 78 to address 130.

124
00:18:11,830 --> 00:18:22,518
Step 1: 130 goes to block B2, the same block as 110. P0's B2 holds 110, so the write misses.

125
00:18:22,968 --> 00:18:32,333
Step 2: the old block, 110, is in M, so it must leave first. The cell M, Repl says: issue PutM, go to I.

126
00:18:32,783 --> 00:18:39,034
PutM writes the dirty block back. Memory 110 becomes 00 30.

127
00:18:39,484 --> 00:18:51,239
Step 3: P0's cell, I with Store, says: issue GetM. GetM 130 goes on the bus. No other cache holds 130, so no cache changes.

128
00:18:51,689 --> 00:19:01,097
Memory sends block 130, 00 30. It fills P0's B2, now with address 130.

129
00:19:01,547 --> 00:19:04,363
P0 follows the edge from I to M.

130
00:19:04,813 --> 00:19:10,594
The store writes 78 into the low word. B2 holds 00 78.

131
00:19:11,044 --> 00:19:22,564
Answer: P0 dot B2 is M, 130, 00 78. Memory 110 is 00 30, from the write-back.

132
00:19:23,014 --> 00:19:33,297
MSI has a cost for private data. A load gets S. So the first store needs a second bus request, GetM, even with no other copy.

133
00:19:33,747 --> 00:19:42,131
Most modern protocols add a fourth state, E, exclusive. It means: I have the only cached copy, and it is clean.

134
00:19:42,581 --> 00:19:51,242
Processor 0 loads A. No other cache holds A, so the load gets E. CPU 0 holds E, with 500.

135
00:19:51,692 --> 00:20:01,420
Processor 0 stores 400. In E, the store needs no bus request. The block changes to M, instantly and silently.

136
00:20:01,870 --> 00:20:08,590
Processor 1 loads A. Processor 0 is in M, so it sends the data and goes to S.

137
00:20:09,040 --> 00:20:16,059
Processor 1 gets S with 400, because a sharer exists. Memory also gets 400.

138
00:20:16,509 --> 00:20:25,149
Processor 1 stores 300. It is in S, so it issues GetM and goes to M. Processor 0 goes to I.

139
00:20:25,599 --> 00:20:34,196
On an eviction, a block in M is dirty and must be written back. A block in E is clean, so its data need not be written back.

140
00:20:34,646 --> 00:20:44,331
So E is the only copy, and it is clean. A load miss with no sharers gets E, and a store to E goes to M with no bus traffic.

141
00:20:44,781 --> 00:20:53,400
The MESI table is the MSI table with a new row, E. The gray cells are the same as in MSI.

142
00:20:53,850 --> 00:21:03,599
One old cell changes. I with Load issues GetS, then goes to S or E. It goes to E when no other processor caches the block.

143
00:21:04,049 --> 00:21:10,065
E with Load is a hit. E with Store is also a hit, and the state changes to M.

144
00:21:10,515 --> 00:21:20,499
E with Repl issues PutM and goes to I. OtherGetS sends the data and goes to S. OtherGetM sends the data and goes to I.

145
00:21:20,949 --> 00:21:29,440
Compare rows E and M. Only the Store cell differs: a store to E needs no request, but the state changes to M.

146
00:21:29,890 --> 00:21:37,015
Here is the MSI diagram with the new state E. The gray edges are the same as in MSI.

147
00:21:37,465 --> 00:21:43,183
From I, a load with no sharers goes to E. A load with sharers still goes to S.

148
00:21:43,633 --> 00:21:45,382
In E, a load hits.

149
00:21:45,832 --> 00:21:49,800
In E, a store goes to M. No request goes on the bus.

150
00:21:50,250 --> 00:21:53,749
In E, OtherGetS sends the data, and goes to S.

151
00:21:54,199 --> 00:22:02,455
In E, OtherGetM sends the data, and goes to I. The table also sends E to I on a replacement, with PutM.

152
00:22:02,905 --> 00:22:11,993
MESI removes the cost of coherence when there is no sharing. So single-threaded programs stay fast on multicores.

153
00:22:12,443 --> 00:22:25,285
Here is the MESI example on a shared bus. All three caches are empty. The shared cache and memory hold A equal to 500, and B equal to 123.

154
00:22:25,735 --> 00:22:30,791
P0 loads A. P0's cache is empty, so the load misses.

155
00:22:31,241 --> 00:22:37,065
P0 is in I with Load: issue GetS. GetS, address A, goes on the bus.

156
00:22:37,515 --> 00:22:42,785
All controllers see it. No other cache holds A, so no cache responds.

157
00:22:43,235 --> 00:22:47,523
So the shared cache responds: address A, data 500.

158
00:22:47,973 --> 00:22:55,311
P0 will get the only copy, and may write it silently. So the shared cache marks A as Modified.

159
00:22:55,761 --> 00:22:57,724
The block travels to P0.

160
00:22:58,174 --> 00:23:06,131
No other cache holds A, so the cell gives E. P0 follows the edge from I to E, and reads 500.

161
00:23:06,581 --> 00:23:12,768
Now P0 stores 600 to A. P0 holds A in E, so the store hits.

162
00:23:13,218 --> 00:23:20,706
The cell E, Store says: hit, go to M. P0 changes to M silently. Nothing goes on the bus.

163
00:23:21,156 --> 00:23:28,537
The store writes 600. The shared cache still holds 500, but it already marked A as Modified.

164
00:23:28,987 --> 00:23:33,489
P1 loads A. P1's cache is empty, so the load misses.

165
00:23:33,939 --> 00:23:41,491
P1's cell, I with Load, says: issue GetS. All controllers see it. For P0, it is an OtherGetS.

166
00:23:41,941 --> 00:23:47,701
P0 holds A in M. The cell M, OtherGetS says: send data, go to S.

167
00:23:48,151 --> 00:23:53,079
P0 puts its data on the bus: address A, data 600.

168
00:23:53,529 --> 00:23:55,470
The block travels to P1.

169
00:23:55,920 --> 00:24:04,176
Another cache holds A, so P1's cell gives S. P1 follows the edge from I to S, and reads 600.

170
00:24:04,626 --> 00:24:07,933
The shared cache takes the data too: 600.

171
00:24:08,383 --> 00:24:15,615
Two cores share A, and memory still holds 500. So the shared cache marks A as Shared, Dirty.

172
00:24:16,065 --> 00:24:24,918
A shared bus cannot scale to large core counts. Every request goes to every core, and the bus carries one request at a time.

173
00:24:25,368 --> 00:24:33,773
Large chips use another interconnect, for example a network-on-chip. A directory protocol replaces the broadcast.

174
00:24:34,223 --> 00:24:46,042
Memory is distributed: each node holds part of the address space, and local accesses are faster. The home of an address holds its memory and its directory entry.

175
00:24:46,492 --> 00:24:57,628
For each block, the directory tracks two things. The owner is the processor with a dirty copy, in M. The sharers are the processors with clean copies, in S.

176
00:24:58,078 --> 00:25:05,310
A processor sends each coherence request to the home directory. The local node is where the request starts.

177
00:25:05,760 --> 00:25:11,904
The home sends messages only to the processors that care. Here, that is the one sharer.

178
00:25:12,354 --> 00:25:20,973
On the cache side, the directory protocol is like bus-based MSI. It has the same three states and the same actions.

179
00:25:21,423 --> 00:25:28,591
But some arcs never trigger an action: in I, OtherGetS and OtherGetM; and in S, OtherGetS.

180
00:25:29,041 --> 00:25:36,763
With a directory, these messages never arrive. The directory does not bother a cache unless the cache must act.

181
00:25:37,213 --> 00:25:52,189
The directory runs its own protocol for each block. Its states are U, uncached, and S, shared, with the set of sharers, S h. The third is M, modified, with the owner, O w. P is the requester.

182
00:25:52,639 --> 00:26:03,157
Its actions: Invalidate goes to all sharers. Fetch gets the data from the owner and sends it home. DataReply sends the data to the requester.

183
00:26:03,607 --> 00:26:11,692
U with GetS: the directory replies with the data. The block goes to S, and the sharer set holds only the requester.

184
00:26:12,142 --> 00:26:17,881
S with GetS: another reader. DataReply, and add the requester to the sharers.

185
00:26:18,331 --> 00:26:23,515
U with GetM: DataReply, go to M. The requester becomes the owner.

186
00:26:23,965 --> 00:26:31,559
S with GetM: invalidate all sharers. The requester becomes the owner, and the sharer set becomes empty.

187
00:26:32,009 --> 00:26:38,601
M with GetS: fetch the data from the owner. The old owner and the requester become the sharers.

188
00:26:39,051 --> 00:26:47,222
M with GetM: fetch the data and invalidate the old owner. Reply with the data. The requester becomes the owner.

189
00:26:47,672 --> 00:26:55,800
PutM from the owner sends the block to U. PutS removes one sharer. When the last sharer leaves, the block goes to U.

190
00:26:56,250 --> 00:27:03,567
The second question type gives the caches and the directory. You give the new states, the directory entry, and memory.

191
00:27:04,017 --> 00:27:11,313
Step 1: the requester checks its line, as before. A miss sends GetS for a load, or GetM for a store.

192
00:27:11,763 --> 00:27:18,931
Step 2: the request goes to the home directory. Read the entry state of the block: U, S or M.

193
00:27:19,381 --> 00:27:28,448
Step 3: the directory acts on its edge. It sends a DataReply from home, fetches from the owner, or invalidates the sharers.

194
00:27:28,898 --> 00:27:34,615
Step 4: each cache that gets a message changes state, by the MSI table.

195
00:27:35,065 --> 00:27:45,028
Step 5: update the directory entry: its state, and the owner or the sharers. Memory changes only when the owner sends data home.

196
00:27:45,478 --> 00:27:58,470
Now the same operations with a directory. P1 holds A in M, with 500. The directory entry of A says Modified, with owner P1. Memory holds 1000.

197
00:27:58,920 --> 00:28:01,245
P0 loads A, and misses.

198
00:28:01,695 --> 00:28:11,658
P0's cell, I with Load, says: issue GetS. P0 sends GetS, address A, to the home directory only. There is no broadcast.

199
00:28:12,108 --> 00:28:16,887
The directory blocks A. Other requests for A wait until this one ends.

200
00:28:17,337 --> 00:28:27,406
The entry says Modified, with owner P1. So the directory forwards the request to P1 only: GetS forward, requester P0.

201
00:28:27,856 --> 00:28:35,323
P1 is in M, and sees a read from another processor. The MSI cell says: send data, go to S.

202
00:28:35,773 --> 00:28:42,173
P1 sends the data straight to P0: response, address A, data 500.

203
00:28:42,623 --> 00:28:45,033
The block arrives in P0's line.

204
00:28:45,483 --> 00:28:49,665
P0 follows I to S, and the load reads 500.

205
00:28:50,115 --> 00:28:56,685
P0 tells the directory that it has the data: unblock, address A, data 500.

206
00:28:57,135 --> 00:29:04,218
The directory takes the data, so its copy of A becomes 500. Memory still holds 1000.

207
00:29:04,668 --> 00:29:15,185
Directory edge M to S: the old owner and the requester become the sharers. The entry is Shared, Dirty, with sharer bits for P0 and P1.

208
00:29:15,635 --> 00:29:22,441
Next, P0 stores 400 to A. P0 holds A in S, so it needs an upgrade.

209
00:29:22,891 --> 00:29:31,531
P0's cell, S with Store, says: issue GetM. P0 sends upgrade GetM, address A, to the home directory.

210
00:29:31,981 --> 00:29:33,922
The directory blocks A again.

211
00:29:34,372 --> 00:29:43,161
The entry is Shared. The directory invalidates every sharer except the requester: here, only P1. Acks equals 1.

212
00:29:43,611 --> 00:29:48,091
P1's MSI cell, S with OtherGetM, says: go to I.

213
00:29:48,541 --> 00:29:56,435
P1 sends its acknowledgement straight to P0. Acks equals 1 told P0 to wait for exactly one.

214
00:29:56,885 --> 00:30:00,810
With all acknowledgements in, P0 follows S to M.

215
00:30:01,260 --> 00:30:03,223
The store writes 400.

216
00:30:03,673 --> 00:30:06,083
P0 unblocks the directory.

217
00:30:06,533 --> 00:30:14,320
Directory edge S to M: P0 is the owner, and no sharers remain. The entry is Modified, Dirty.

218
00:30:14,770 --> 00:30:26,397
Before: P1 owned A. After: P0 owns A with 400, and P1 is invalid. The directory sent each message only to the caches that hold A.

219
00:30:26,847 --> 00:30:35,743
MESI with a directory. P1 holds A in S. Block B is uncached: its entry is Idle, with no sharers.

220
00:30:36,193 --> 00:30:38,475
P0 loads B, and misses.

221
00:30:38,925 --> 00:30:43,064
P0 sends GetS, address B, to the home directory.

222
00:30:43,514 --> 00:30:45,178
The directory blocks B.

223
00:30:45,628 --> 00:30:54,929
The entry of B is Idle: no cache holds B. So the directory replies with the data: address B, data 123.

224
00:30:55,379 --> 00:30:57,790
The block arrives in P0's line.

225
00:30:58,240 --> 00:31:06,411
No other cache holds B, so P0 gets E. P0 follows I to E, and reads 123.

226
00:31:06,861 --> 00:31:11,725
P0 unblocks the directory, with the data 123.

227
00:31:12,175 --> 00:31:21,028
A block in E can change to M with no message. So the directory records P0 as the owner: P0 is Modified.

228
00:31:21,478 --> 00:31:28,625
Next, P0 stores 456 to B. P0 holds B in E, so the store hits.

229
00:31:29,075 --> 00:31:34,109
E with Store: go to M, silently. No message goes to the directory.

230
00:31:34,559 --> 00:31:42,154
The store writes 456. The directory is still correct: it already lists P0 as the owner.

231
00:31:42,604 --> 00:31:51,457
Assignment 8, question 1 b, uses the same caches with a directory. Memory holds a directory entry for each block.

232
00:31:51,907 --> 00:31:56,579
The states DI, DS and DM are the slides' U, S and M.

233
00:31:57,029 --> 00:32:07,760
The entry also lists the owner or the sharers. Memory is not updated on a write to a block that is M in another cache. Each part starts from this state.

234
00:32:08,210 --> 00:32:16,978
Pause the video and try it. Part 6: P0 reads 120, then P1 writes 80 to 120.

235
00:32:17,428 --> 00:32:28,457
First, P0 reads 120. B0 holds 100 in I, so it misses. P0 sends GetS 120 to the home.

236
00:32:28,907 --> 00:32:39,681
The entry is DS, with sharer P15. Edge S, GetS: DataReply. The data, 00 20, goes to P0's B0.

237
00:32:40,131 --> 00:32:42,307
P0 follows I to S.

238
00:32:42,757 --> 00:32:49,327
The directory adds P0. The entry is DS, with sharers P15 and P0.

239
00:32:49,777 --> 00:33:00,636
Next, P1 writes 120. P1's B0 holds 100 in I: a miss. P1 sends GetM 120 home.

240
00:33:01,086 --> 00:33:07,657
The entry is DS. Edge S, GetM: invalidate all sharers. P15 goes to I.

241
00:33:08,107 --> 00:33:11,904
P0 is a sharer too, so P0 goes to I.

242
00:33:12,354 --> 00:33:20,845
P1 has no copy, so the directory also sends the data. 00 20 fills P1's B0.

243
00:33:21,295 --> 00:33:23,129
P1 follows I to M.

244
00:33:23,579 --> 00:33:28,443
P1 writes 80. B0 holds 00 80.

245
00:33:28,893 --> 00:33:35,187
The entry becomes DM, with owner P1. Memory keeps 00 20.

246
00:33:35,637 --> 00:33:59,338
Answer: P0 dot B0 is I, 120, 00 20. P15 dot B0 is I, 120, 00 20. P1 dot B0 is M, 120, 00 80. The entry is DM, 120, owner P1, data 00 20.

247
00:33:59,788 --> 00:34:08,641
Pause the video and try it. Part 7: P0 writes 80 to 120, then P1 reads 120.

248
00:34:09,091 --> 00:34:19,523
First, P0 writes 120. B0 holds 100 in I: a miss. P0 sends GetM 120 home.

249
00:34:19,973 --> 00:34:28,315
The entry is DS, with sharer P15. Edge S, GetM: invalidate P15. P15 goes to I.

250
00:34:28,765 --> 00:34:37,063
P0 has no copy, so the directory sends the data. 00 20 fills P0's B0.

251
00:34:37,513 --> 00:34:39,732
P0 follows I to M.

252
00:34:40,182 --> 00:34:45,259
P0 writes 80. B0 holds 00 80.

253
00:34:45,709 --> 00:34:49,080
The entry becomes DM, with owner P0.

254
00:34:49,530 --> 00:34:55,951
Next, P1 reads 120: a miss. P1 sends GetS 120 home.

255
00:34:56,401 --> 00:35:04,721
The entry is DM, with owner P0. Edge M, GetS: fetch from the owner. P0 follows M to S.

256
00:35:05,171 --> 00:35:11,571
Fetch sends P0's data home. Memory 120 becomes 00 80.

257
00:35:12,021 --> 00:35:16,693
DataReply sends 00 80 to P1's B0.

258
00:35:17,143 --> 00:35:18,978
P1 follows I to S.

259
00:35:19,428 --> 00:35:28,004
Edge M to S: the old owner and the requester become the sharers. The entry is DS, with P0 and P1.

260
00:35:28,454 --> 00:35:47,697
Answer: P0 dot B0 and P1 dot B0 are S, 120, 00 80. P15 dot B0 is I. The entry is DS, 120, sharers P0 and P1, data 00 80.

261
00:35:48,147 --> 00:35:57,853
Pause the video and try it. Part 8: P0 writes 80 to 120, then P1 writes 90 to 120.

262
00:35:58,303 --> 00:36:10,485
The first write is the same as in part 7. P15 goes to I, P0 holds M with 00 80, and the entry is DM with owner P0.

263
00:36:10,935 --> 00:36:17,292
Next, P1 writes 120: a miss. P1 sends GetM 120 home.

264
00:36:17,742 --> 00:36:25,785
The entry is DM, with owner P0. Edge M, GetM: fetch and invalidate. P0 goes to I.

265
00:36:26,235 --> 00:36:32,037
P0's data, 00 80, goes on to P1's B0.

266
00:36:32,487 --> 00:36:34,322
P1 follows I to M.

267
00:36:34,772 --> 00:36:39,892
P1 writes 90. B0 holds 00 90.

268
00:36:40,342 --> 00:36:48,022
The entry becomes DM, with owner P1. Memory is not updated: it keeps 00 20.

269
00:36:48,472 --> 00:37:09,912
Answer: P0 dot B0 is I, 120, 00 80. P1 dot B0 is M, 120, 00 90. P15 dot B0 is I. The entry is DM, 120, owner P1, data 00 20.

270
00:37:10,362 --> 00:37:22,245
In MSI and MESI, a GetS to a block in M or E sends the data to two places. One is the requester. The other is the memory controller.

271
00:37:22,695 --> 00:37:31,804
The shared cache must take the copy, because it becomes the owner of the block. It answers later requests while the block is in I or S.

272
00:37:32,254 --> 00:37:38,931
The problem: when the block is written again soon, each of these writes to the shared cache is unnecessary.

273
00:37:39,381 --> 00:37:48,853
One solution adds an owner state, O. On OtherGetS in M or E, the cache sends the data only to the requester, and goes to O.

274
00:37:49,303 --> 00:37:58,754
The O cache becomes the owner. It answers reads of the block, and it writes the block back when it is evicted. The shared cache is not updated.

275
00:37:59,204 --> 00:38:03,684
With the owner state O, the protocol is called MOESI.

276
00:38:04,134 --> 00:38:17,595
Pause the video and try it. Assignment 8, question 2: add the state O, owned, to MESI, and draw the MOESI diagram. Gray edges are from MESI.

277
00:38:18,045 --> 00:38:29,736
In A8, a block in M goes to O on OtherGetS. It sends the data only to the requester. This edge replaces the MESI edge from M to S.

278
00:38:30,186 --> 00:38:37,546
In O, a load hits. An OtherGetS keeps the state O: the O cache sends the dirty data to the reader.

279
00:38:37,996 --> 00:38:44,759
In O, a local store needs the only copy. The cache invalidates the sharers, and goes to M.

280
00:38:45,209 --> 00:38:53,699
In O, OtherGetM invalidates the block. A replacement writes the block back, because O is the owner. Both go to I.

281
00:38:54,149 --> 00:39:05,115
Other caches with the block stay in S. E keeps its MESI edges, as in the posted solution. The slide also allows E to go to O.

282
00:39:05,565 --> 00:39:17,341
To recap. The coherence controller reacts to local loads, stores and replacements, and to OtherGetS and OtherGetM from the bus. Each protocol is a table.

283
00:39:17,791 --> 00:39:30,932
VI allows one copy. MSI allows many clean copies or one dirty copy. MESI adds E, the only clean copy, so a store to E needs no bus request.

284
00:39:31,382 --> 00:39:41,729
A snooping trace has five steps: find the line, replace, request, the other caches, then fill, write and memory. Use the table cell at every step.

285
00:39:42,179 --> 00:39:51,800
A directory trace sends each request home. The entry state picks the edge, and the directory messages only the caches that hold the block.

286
00:39:52,250 --> 00:40:06,117
MOESI adds O, so a shared dirty block does not go to the shared cache each time. That is the end of video 22b. Video 22c is about memory consistency.
